    "doc_id": "2025-08-04-islandlk-97023050",
    "num": "islandlk-97023050",
    "date_str": "2025-08-04",
    "description": "Extra-curricular activities: Are they losing their purpose?",
    "url_metadata": "http://island.lk/extra-curricular-activities-are-they-losing-their-purpose/",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "islandlk",
    "time_ut": 1754329980.0,
    "chunk_id": "2025-08-04-islandlk-97023050-0000",
    "chunk_index": 0,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "1fe77ed822871413ddd4e21827aba7c7",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1235,
    "chunk_text": "Extra-curricular activities: Are they losing their purpose?\n\nThe school is a place that is not restricted to formal, classroom learning. It is also a place where students explore, develop and hone their interests in sports, literature and arts. Schools pay special attention to these extra-curricular activities as they are essential for recreational purposes. By participating in extra-curricular activities, students cultivate a long-lasting passion for creativity and a deep interest in collaborating with others. Even though extra-curricular activities offer all these benefits, I find the way many schools and the Department of Education, especially in the Jaffna district, conduct these activities problematic and disappointing. This Kuppi Talk article raises the question whether the manner in which these activities are conducted and the increase in the time allocated to these activities, sometimes at the expense of required classroom learning, is yielding the desired outcome. Observing that extra-curricular activities are now narrowly understood as competitions, the article unpacks the colonialist ideologies that undergird some of them and calls for a renewed approach that lays emphasis on creativity and collaboration."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
