    "md5": "737fa0ba1831afc2f41bcb06f9693ed2",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1803,
    "chunk_text": "Critics initially argued that the government had taken too long to respond and was not deploying its best negotiating talent. However, the outcome speaks for itself. Through discreet diplomacy and a non-confrontational approach, the delegation succeeded in winning vital trade space for Sri Lankan exporters. This unexpected win is a public relations success for the government and likely to improve confidence in its ability to manage the economy under difficult global conditions.\u00a0Indeed, the Colombo Stock Exchange reflected this boost in confidence, registering a new all-time high even before the tariff reduction was confirmed and continuing with its bull run. The buoyancy of the stock market indicates that the business elite and top investors believe that the government is taking decisions that safeguard their interests.\n\nSri Lanka remains a country divided between a minority living in relative luxury often in urban centres and a majority struggling to meet basic needs. This two-tiered structure exists not only in the economic domain but also across social, cultural, and political spheres.\u00a0 More than any other leadership in Sri Lanka since Independence, the present government leadership has experienced this dual structure from the bottom tier and appears committed to transforming this reality.\u00a0 \u00a0It is in this broader context that the concept of inclusion takes on added urgency.\u00a0 Efforts to build a more inclusive society need to extend beyond economic policy.\u00a0 An example would be a consultation held by the Ministry of Mass Media on a proposed National Media Policy. This is part of a broader series of initiatives being supported by the United Nations Development Programme (UNDP) in Sri Lanka, which has encouraged civil society participation in policy drafting processes."
