    "chunk_text": "SL warned of costly economic consequences from US\u2019 new tariff policies\n\nHuman rights and policy analyst Ranjith Keerthi Tennakoon has warned that new U.S. tariff policies under the Trump administration could have profound and far-reaching consequences for Sri Lanka\u2019s fragile economy \u2014 particularly in apparel exports, mineral sales, and trade relations.\n\nSpeaking to The Island Financial Review, Tennakoon, Executive Director of the Sri Lanka Human Rights Organization, called for transparency and clarity regarding the \u201ceconomic and security partnerships\u201d being negotiated between Sri Lanka and the United States, which he says remain veiled from public scrutiny.\n\n\u201cThe government must clearly disclose the terms of these economic and defense linkages. These agreements will shape our national interests \u2014 especially in energy, trade and mineral exports. The lack of transparency is unacceptable, Tennakoon stated.\n\nAccording to the policy briefing issued on August 3, the Trump administration\u2019s revised tariff strategy \u2014 branded by some as protectionist and transactional \u2014 has recalibrated global trade dynamics. While the U.S. maintains preferential tax rates for 48 countries, Sri Lanka faces taxes from 15 U.S. states, with only four maintaining parity.\n\nMore alarmingly, Tennakoon emphasized that this situation leaves Sri Lanka at a competitive disadvantage, especially against regional rivals who continue to benefit from lower or zero-duty status.\n\n\u201cSri Lanka is essentially being penalized. We now compete on unequal footing with countries that enjoy better tariff concessions. Our garments, for instance, face higher duties, making us less attractive to U.S. buyers, he explained.\n\nThe policy paper reveals that Sri Lanka had previously enjoyed a trade surplus with the U.S., with Sri Lankan exports reaching USD 3.37 billion, compared to USD 738 million in imports. However, exports have now slumped:"
