    "chunk_size_bytes": 1904,
    "chunk_text": "All those who abused power to amass wealth and perpetrate various crimes, including the physical elimination of some of their political rivals, during previous governments, must be made to face the full force of the law. However, if these characters succeed in making the ongoing probes and legal action against them out to be a political witch-hunt, the NPP government\u2019s efforts to have them thrown behind bars and relegated to the political dustbin may come a cropper. One may recall that there were many arrests during the  Yahapalana government (2015-2019), which also made its political opponents languish in remand prison for months. But such action did not yield the desired results; most of the hurriedly filed cases collapsed, and the villains became heroes; the defeated rulers made a stunning comeback in 2018.\n\nSoon after the Prime Minister\u2019s statement in Parliament yesterday, SJB MP Chaminda Wijesiri took the floor, tearing into the government. Welcoming legal action against those who were facing serious allegations of bribery, corruption and abuse of power, he demanded to know why some ruling party members who were responsible for several mega scandals had been allowed to remain above the law. He said the questionable release of 323 freight containers from the Colombo Port without Customs checks in January was as bad as the Treasury bond scams under the Yahapalana government. (The irony of his allegation may not have been lost on keen political observers. The SJB members were in the UNP, which led the Yahapalana government, and Wijesiri\u2019s allegation is tantamount to a self-indictment.) He likened the salt scandal under the incumbent government to the Sathosa garlic racket during the SLPP administration. He also asked whether any action would be taken against a company that had secured a wind farm contract in a questionable manner by leveraging its political clout."
