    "chunk_size_bytes": 1861,
    "chunk_text": "Asia is the growth hub of the world and the West is in Decline at this time. South and Southeast Asian markets already have similar products, so targeting wealthy Central and West Asian markets where transport costs would be manageable makes sense. Sri Lankan businesses also need to pivot to the BRICS and their allies to grow the economy- particularly China and Russia.\n\nThe Trump Tariff \u2018shock\u201d discourse in the corporate media echo chamber and among think tanks have been narrowly focused on negotiations with Washington by the Sri Lanka team based on hyped up disaster scenarios. There has been inadequate analysis based on accurate data on Sri Lanka\u2019s export products and markets profile, regarding the real impacts of the Trump tariff hike.\n\nWhile the US makes claims that it is the biggest export market of Sri Lankan goods and hence the biggest contributor to foreign exchange earnings, the European Union (EU) has made a similar claim!\n\nBy making such claims, both the EU and US seek to exert influence in the country, to control its development trajectory and resources and shape the geostrategic island\u2019s (under) development outcomes.\n\nAt a recent discussion at the Center for Poverty Analysis it was surmised that while the Trump tariffs would have marginal impact on tea, rubber and coconut export sectors as these go to other markets, women in the garment sector were the most vulnerable and likely to face hardship.\n\nData accuracy regarding the volume of exports and their markets are of concern in the context of evaluating any impact of the Tariff Shock. This also in the context of concerns about some private sector firms in the apparel sector that are heavily import dependent, under-invoicing, tax evading and parking foreign currency overseas which also contributed to the staged first ever Sovereign Default of Sri Lanka in 2022."
