    "md5": "a3cf30aac79a958c210dfc551fd8674e",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1854,
    "chunk_text": "The JVP-led NPP has a two-thirds majority in Parliament, and therefore the no-faith motion against Jayasekera has no chance of passage. However, the Opposition will be able to make the government either defend Jayasekera or ask him to resign as the Deputy Minister of Defence. The SJB and its allies have built a strong case against Jayasekera, and it will be a huge political gamble for the government to throw its weight behind him. The Catholic Church has also expressed serious concern about his holding the post of Deputy Defence Minister during investigations into the Easter Sunday terror attacks. So, the government finds itself in a dilemma. It cannot throw Jayasekera to the wolves by asking him to resign, for such action will amount to a victory for the Opposition.\n\nThe SJB ought to ask itself whether its leaders have any moral right to condemn others for failure to prevent the Easter Sunday terror attacks. The SJB leaders were members of the UNP-led Yahapalana government, which the Presidential Commission of Inquiry that probed the Easter Sunday terror attacks has held accountable. The commission report says, \u201cThe dysfunctional Government was a major contributory factor for the events that took place on 21st April 2019. The Government including President [Maithripala] Sirisena and Prime Minister [Ranil Wickremesinghe] is accountable for the tragedy.\u201d (pp. 470-71) The SJB leaders, while in their UNP days, campaigned hard for Sirisena in the 2015 presidential race, and fought quite a battle to prevent the ouster of PM Wickremesinghe during what came to be dubbed the 2018 constitutional coup. Therefore, before moving the no-faith motion against Jayasekera, the holier-than-thou SJB seniors should apologise to the public for their contribution to the Yahapalana government\u2019s lapses that led to the Easter Sunday attacks."
  },
