    "md5": "269cb2e5d3746c34b788f207f24aec97",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1932,
    "chunk_text": "With a sizable portion of GDP coming from the informal sector, stablecoins could make GST and income tax collection harder.\n\nTerrorist financing and illicit arms procurement could become harder to detect, especially if wallets are created and traded outside India\u2019s jurisdiction.\n\nFor Sri Lanka, still recovering from a severe debt crisis and IMF-led restructuring, the stakes are different but equally urgent:\n\nWidespread use of dollar-backed stablecoins could accelerate the partial dollarisation of the economy, undermining confidence in the rupee.\n\nWith limited foreign reserves and tight import controls, stablecoins offer a discreet way to move dollars offshore.\n\nThe Central Bank of Sri Lanka lacks the manpower and technical infrastructure to monitor decentralised blockchain transactions effectively.\n\nGiven Sri Lanka\u2019s reliance on tourism, remittances, and aid flows, the widespread use of US-issued stablecoins could weaken exchange rate stability and make monetary policy less effective.\n\nIf the US becomes the \u201cSwitzerland of crypto\u201d, as some critics suggest, South Asian countries will face a dilemma:\n\n= Either adopt and regulate stablecoins aggressively, investing heavily in compliance infrastructure,\n\n= Or restrict and ban their use \u2014 a move that risks pushing them underground, just as happened with hawala networks and unlicensed moneylenders.\n\nWhat is clear is that regulation-lite stablecoins from the US could function as a form of \u201csuper cash\u201d \u2014 offering all the convenience of mobile payments with the anonymity of banknotes, but in a form that transcends borders.\n\nFor South Asia, the challenge is urgent: craft a coordinated regulatory strategy, invest in blockchain analytic and consider region-wide frameworks under SAARC or BIMSTEC. Otherwise, the benefits of crypto innovation may be outweighed by an erosion of tax revenues, financial sovereignty, and monetary control. (See Figure 1"
