    "chunk_text": "Sri Lanka stocks surge 1.25-pct, led by banks\n\nECONOMYNEXT \u2013 The Colombo Stock Exchange\u2019s All Share Price Index (ASPI) continued to trade higher surpassing the 20,400 mark on Monday, data showed.\n\n\u201cMarket was very positive today. Investor interest in the banking sector was seen. Almost all the banks went up,\u201d Ranjan Ranatunga, Assistant Vice President \u2013 Research at First Capital said.\n\nThe ASPI rose 1.25 percent, or 252.16 points higher from Friday to close at 20,470.52; the S&P SL20 index climbed 1.76 percent, or 103.60 points, to close at 5,998.44.\n\n\u201cBanking sector rose on positive quarterly results,\u201d Ranatunga said. \u201cInterest was also seen in retail and high net worth shares.\u201d\n\nThe ASPI was pushed higher on Commercial Bank which ended 7.75 rupees stronger at 189; Carson Cumberbatch which rose 105.50 rupees to 746; Melstacorp which ended 5 rupees up at 165; John Keells Holdings which moved 50 cents up to 23.50 rupees; and DFCC Bank which closed 5.75 rupees higher at 166.\n\nTurnover fell to 7.7 billion rupees from 9.5 billion rupees, while the share volume was 247,608,865.\n\n\u201cTurnover was very good today. It was above the monthly average of 6.4 billion rupees.\u201d\n\nElsewhere, Tokyo\u2019s Nikkei 225 index surpassed the psychological 43,700 mark hitting record high levels for the second consecutive session as investor optimism surrounding domestic economy and hopes for an interest rate cut by the US Federal Reserve were expected, Japan\u2019s The Mainichi newspaper said.\n\nThe index moved 0.77 percent, or 336.00 points higher, to 43,714.31.\n\nThe Pakistan Stock Exchange\u2019s KSE 100 Index was trading 1.03 percent stronger at 148,003.52.\n\nMeanwhile, India\u2019s Nifty 50 rose 1.00 percent to 24,876.95, while the BSE Sensex was 0.84 percent up at 81,273.75.\n\nAs at 3.35 pm Sri Lankan time, spot gold was trading at 3,363.03 US dollars, up 19.75 US dollars. (Colombo/Aug18/2025)"
