    "md5": "2272cf4e538e8460c988800f193f20ce",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1878,
    "chunk_text": "No-faith motion and double bind\n\nSpeaker Dr. Jagath Wickramaratne yesterday informed Parliament that he needed more time to study the no-faith motion, submitted by the Opposition against Deputy Minister of Defence Maj. Gen. (retd.) Aruna Jayasekera, and reveal his opinion thereon. Some government bigwigs have already declared that a no-faith motion cannot be moved against a deputy minister, and therefore speculation is rife in political circles that the Opposition\u2019s efforts will be in vain. However, the issue the Opposition has raised through the no-faith motion will not go away.\n\nThe government\u2019s argument that no-confidence motions cannot be moved against deputy ministers does not have a leg to stand on. The Constitution and the Standing Orders do not say that a no-faith motion cannot be moved against a deputy minister, as some legal experts have rightly pointed out. It is hoped that the government will not throw away the rule book, and bulldoze its way through.\n\nIn a bid to bolster its claim that no-faith motions can be moved not only against ministers but also against others, the SJB has cited what President J. R. Jayewardene\u2019s government did to TULF leader A. Amirthalingam in 1981. This argument, we believe, is self-defeating. A motion of no confidence was moved against the then Opposition Leader Amirthalingam, but its passage had no effect on him, as he did not have to command the confidence of the entire Parliament to function as the Opposition Leader; he only had to enjoy the confidence of the Opposition. That unprecedented, despicable no-confidence motion, which irreparably damaged the reputation of the Parliament of Sri Lanka, cannot be considered a precedent worthy of emulation by any stretch of the imagination. That said, it will be a mistake for the NPP government not to take up the no-faith motion against Jayasekera for debate."
  },
