    "chunk_text": "Sri Lanka stands at a critical juncture. The path forward demands more than mere tinkering with curricula or superficial policy adjustments. It requires a fundamental paradigm shift\u2014a courageous reimagining of what education is and what it can achieve. This is not simply about reforming a system; it is about redefining a nation\u2019s future. The journey begins with a candid and uncomfortable examination of the deep-seated issues that have led to this point of stagnation. It necessitates a move away from the politically expedient but ultimately destructive culture of \u201ceducation for free\u201d towards a more sustainable and empowering model of \u201cfree education.\u201d This essay argues for a comprehensive overhaul of the Sri Lankan education system, one that is rooted in principles of equity, innovation, and genuine empowerment, and is designed to unlock the nation\u2019s most valuable resource: its human talent.\n\nIn the landscape of representative democracy, the promise of \u201cfree\u201d services became a potent tool for securing electoral mandates. Politicians of all stripes have consistently exploited the genuine aspirations of the people, offering education and healthcare as entitlements in exchange for votes. This has cultivated a pervasive \u201centitlement culture,\u201d a societal mindset where essential services are viewed not as a shared responsibility but as a right to be passively received from the state. The narrative of \u201cfree education\u201d has been a particularly powerful and emotive one, yet it has masked a debilitating reality. What has been delivered is not true \u201cfree education\u201d\u2014a system that liberates and empowers\u2014but \u201ceducation for free,\u201d a system that fosters dependency and is paid for by the very people it claims to serve, through a regressive system of indirect taxation that disproportionately burdens the poor and marginalised."
  },
