    "chunk_text": "In the landscape of representative democracy, the promise of \u201cfree\u201d services became a potent tool for securing electoral mandates. Politicians of all stripes have consistently exploited the genuine aspirations of the people, offering education and healthcare as entitlements in exchange for votes. This has cultivated a pervasive \u201centitlement culture,\u201d a societal mindset where essential services are viewed not as a shared responsibility but as a right to be passively received from the state. The narrative of \u201cfree education\u201d has been a particularly powerful and emotive one, yet it has masked a debilitating reality. What has been delivered is not true \u201cfree education\u201d\u2014a system that liberates and empowers\u2014but \u201ceducation for free,\u201d a system that fosters dependency and is paid for by the very people it claims to serve, through a regressive system of indirect taxation that disproportionately burdens the poor and marginalised.\n\nAt the heart of Sri Lanka\u2019s educational malaise lies a fundamental misunderstanding\u2014a conflation of two profoundly different concepts: \u201cfree education\u201d and \u201ceducation for free.\u201d This is not a mere semantic distinction; it is a philosophical chasm that defines the very purpose and potential of an education system."
