    "chunk_size_bytes": 1915,
    "chunk_text": "Sri Lanka\u2019s plantation giants embrace regenerative agriculture in landmark shift\n\nAgreements with global sustainability body set to transform 36,000 hectares of tea\n\nIn a transformative move for the nation\u2019s plantation economy, four of Sri Lanka\u2019s leading agricultural companies recently signed landmark agreements in Colombo to adopt regenerative farming practices. The partnerships with Solidaridad Network Asia and its local partner, Nucleus Foundation, will position the island\u2019s crucial tea sector to access valuable carbon markets and meet stringent new EU import regulations.\n\nHayleys Plantations, Aitken Spence\u2019s Elpitiya Plantations PLC, and Lumbini Tea Valley Ceylon will implement the globally recognised Regenagri standard across nearly 36,000 hectares of tea estates. One of the largest initiatives of its kind in Sri Lanka, it directly addresses declining yields and climate vulnerability by integrating traditional wisdom with modern technology.\n\nThe Regenagri framework will enable companies to generate and trade Carbon Removal Units (CRUs), creating a direct economic incentive for sustainable land management. This is strategically vital for complying with the European Union\u2019s Deforestation Regulation (EUDR) and Corporate Sustainability Due Diligence Directive (CSDDD).\n\n\u201cSustainable development is our core policy,\u201d said Hayleys Plantations Managing Director Dr. Roshan Rajadurai at a press conference following the signing of its MoU with the global sustainability body on August 12. \u201cThis framework allows us to preserve our environment by blending ancient practices with modern techniques for the benefit of both the land and our workforce.\u201d\n\nDr. Shatadru Chattopadhayay, Managing Director of Solidaridad Asia, stated, \u201cThese MoUs mark a decisive shift, moving beyond mere compliance to build climate-resilient value chains prepared for evolving global regulations.\u201d"
